in. \n\nWait, perhaps I miscalculated 61×5. Let me recalculate 61×5 again. 60×5 is 300, plus 1×5 is 5, so 305. That\'s correct. So 61×50 is 3050. \n\n61×3 is 183. 3050 + 183: 3050 + 180 is 3230, plus 3 is 3233. \n\nWait, maybe the user\'s initial thought was wrong. Let me confirm using another method. \n\nAlternatively, let\'s do 61×53. \n\nAnother way: 60×50 = 3000, 60×3=180, 1×50=50, 1×3=3. \n\nAdding all those together: 3000 + 180 = 3180, plus 50 is 3230, plus 3 is 3233. \n\nSo the correct answer should be 3233. But the user wrote that they think it\'s 3213. Maybe they made an error in their calculation? Let me see what 61×53 actually is. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps the user has a typo, but according to the problem given, it\'s 61×53. Let me do it step by step again. \n\nFirst, 61 ×3: \n\n3 ×1 =3, 3×60=180. 180+3=183. \n\nThen 61 ×50: \n\n50×60=3000, 50×1=50. 3000+50=3050. \n\nAdding 183 + 3050: \n\n3050 + 183. Let\'s add 3000 + 100 = 3100, 50 +80=130, 3+3=6. Wait, no, that\'s not the right way. Let me add them properly:\n\n3050  \n+ 183  \n= ?\n\nStarting from the right: 0 +3 =3  \n5 +8=13 (write down 3, carryover 1)  \n0 +1 +1 (carryover) =2  \n3 + nothing =3 \n\nSo that gives 3233. \n\nHmm. So the correct answer should be 3233, not 3213. The user\'s initial thought might be incorrect. Maybe they did 60×53=3180, then added 1×53=53, getting 3180+53=3233. Wait, that\'s the same. Alternatively, maybe they did 61×50=3050 and 61×3=183, but added wrong. 3050 + 183: 3050 + 100 is 3150, plus 83 is 3233. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps there was a miscalculation in their head. For instance, if they thought 61×50 is 3000 (60×50=3000) but then added 1×50=50, making 3050, but then maybe added 61×3 as 180 instead of 183, getting 3050+180=3230, then plus 3? Wait, no. Alternatively, maybe they thought 61×3 is 180, so 3050+180=3230. But that would be 3230, not 3213. Hmm. Alternatively, maybe they miscalculated 61×53 as 60×50=3000, plus 60×3=180, plus 1×50=50, plus 1×3=3. Then 3000+1